# Vercel Speed Insights Installation Report

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js project following the latest official documentation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- **File**: `package.json`
- **Action**: Added `@vercel/speed-insights` version `^2.0.0` as a dependency
- **Command Used**: `pnpm i @vercel/speed-insights`

### 2. Component Configuration
- **File**: `src/app/layout.tsx`
- **Changes**:
  - Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next";`
  - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component at the end of the `<body>` tag in the root layout
  - This follows the official Next.js v13.5+ integration pattern from Vercel's documentation

## Implementation Details

The integration follows the official Vercel Speed Insights quickstart guide (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart):

1. Installed the package using the project's package manager (pnpm)
2. Imported the SpeedInsights component from `@vercel/speed-insights/next` 
3. Placed the component in the root layout file within the body tag
4. This placement ensures Speed Insights tracking is available across all pages

## Verification

- ✅ Package successfully installed
- ✅ Layout.tsx passes ESLint with no errors
- ✅ TypeScript imports are correctly typed
- ✅ Code structure preserved, only necessary changes made
- ✅ Changes follow Next.js App Router conventions

## Next Steps

To fully enable Speed Insights:

1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Navigate to Speed Insights in the Vercel dashboard
3. Select the project and click "Enable"
4. After deployment and user visits, performance metrics will appear in the dashboard

## Notes

- The SpeedInsights component is lightweight and will only load tracking scripts when deployed on Vercel
- In local development, the component is essentially a no-op
- The build process showed pre-existing environment variable validation errors (not related to this change)
- All existing code structure and functionality has been preserved
